The proposed solution correctly identifies the range of possible square roots (32 to 99) and correctly determines that the root itself must be a multiple of 6, ranging from \(6 \cdot 6 = 36\) to \(6 \cdot 16 = 96\). 

However, in the final step, the solution commits an "off-by-one" (fencepost) error when counting the number of multiples. To count the integers from 6 to 16 inclusive, one must calculate \(16 - 6 + 1 = 11\), not \(16 - 6 = 10\).

