The proposed solution is incorrect because it fails to apply the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion. While it correctly counts the number of integers for which $n^2 + 1$ is divisible by 5 (400) and the number of integers for which $n^2 + 1$ is divisible by 13 (154), it simply adds these two counts together without subtracting the overlap—the integers for which $n^2 + 1$ is divisible by both 5 and 13 (i.e., divisible by 65). 

By the Chinese Remainder Theorem, $n^2 \equiv -1 \pmod{65}$ has $2 \times 2 = 4$ solutions modulo 65, specifically $n \equiv 8, 18, 47, 57 \pmod{65}$. Since $1000 = 65 \times 15 + 25$, each of the 4 residues appears 15 times up to 975 (giving $15 \times 4 = 60$ integers), and two of the residues (8 and 18) fall within the remaining range of 25. Thus, there are $60 + 2 = 62$ overlapping integers that were double-counted. Subtracting this overlap gives $554 - 62 = 492$.

